eline_func includes try-except blocks and logging to manage errors effectively. Function calling in LLM applications enables dynamic interactions with external systems through structured API integrations. Proper implementation requires defining function declarations with specific parameters, such as those used with the Frankfurter API for currency exchange, and adhering to 2026 real-time operation standards. Grouping functions by business logic in Gemini API v3.5 improves maintainability and reduces development complexity. To ensure reliability, use DeepEval v2.6 for automated testing of function parameters like currency_from and currency_to. For production systems, prioritize unit testing with Python’s unittest framework and evaluate performance using Zilliz’s 2026 case study benchmarks A best practice is to maintain a test suite that runs automatically with each system update. This ensures that any changes are validated against a broad set of use cases, reducing the risk of introducing errors. By combining unit testing, functional testing, evaluation metrics, and regression testing, developers can build robust and reliable function calling systems that deliver accurate results and adapt to evolving requirements. Function calling in LLM applications enables dynamic interactions with external systems through structured API integrations. Proper implementation requires defining function declarations with specific parameters, such as those used with the Frankfurter API for currency exchange, and adhering to 2026 real-time operation standards. Grouping functions by business logic in Gemini API v3.5 improves maintainability and reduces development complexity Group Functions by Business Logic or Purpose #\n\nAfter the LLM generates a response, the next step is to extract the parameters from the function call. This is typically done by parsing the response object and extracting the function name and arguments. For example, in the Gemini API, the parameters can be extracted as follows:\nHere, the keys are adjusted to match the expected parameter names. This step is crucial for ensuring that the extracted parameters align with the function declaration’s requirements. You can verify the correctness of the parameters by logging them or passing them to a validation function.\nOnce the parameters are extracted, the function is executed, and the results are returned to the LLM. For example, using the Frankfurter API (version 2.0.1), the exchange rate can be fetched as follows:\n\nimport requests\n\nurl = f\"https://api.frankfurter.app/{params['date']}\"\napi_response = requests.get(url).json()\n\n\nThe API response is then used to generate a final response for the user. This process can be repeated if additional data is required, allowing for complex interactions and workflows. The LLM can call multiple functions in a single turn or in sequence, depending on the application’s requirements.\nThis structured approach ensures that LLMs can effectively interact with external systems, providing accurate and dynamic responses based on real-time data. Best practices from 2026 emphasize using well-defined function schemas, validating parameters before execution, and implementing robust error handling for external API calls.\nOrganizing functions by business logic or purpose enhances readability, maintainability, and scalability of systems. In modern LLM applications, such as those using the Gemini API (version 3.5), grouping functions ensures that developers can quickly locate and modify specific modules without affecting unrelated components. For example, in a financial system, functions like get_exchange_rate, calculate_interest, and validate_transaction can be grouped under a Finance module. This approach reduces cognitive load and improves collaboration among teams. This process can be repeated if additional data is required, allowing for complex interactions and workflows. The LLM can call multiple functions in a single turn or in sequence, depending on the application’s requirements. This structured approach ensures that LLMs can effectively interact with external systems, providing accurate and dynamic responses based on real-time data. Best practices from 2026 emphasize using well-defined function schemas, validating parameters before execution, and implementing robust error handling for external API calls. Best Practices for Function Calling #. Group Functions by Business Logic or Purpose #. Organizing functions by business logic or purpose enhances readability, maintainability, and scalability of systems. … Function calling is a powerful feature that enables LLMs to interact with external systems and perform dynamic operations. By integrating with external tools, LLMs can deliver more accurate, current, and relevant responses, making them more effective for a wide range of applications. As the technology continues to evolve, the importance of function calling in LLMs will only grow, offering new opportunities for developers and businesses alike. Technical Implementation of Function Calling #. Function calling in large language models (LLMs) enables dynamic interaction with external tools, databases, and APIs. This capability allows LLMs to perform real-time operations beyond static text generation As of 2026, function calling is a standard feature in many advanced LLMs, including Google’s Gemini series and OpenAI’s GPT-4 and GPT-3.5 models. The latest version of the Gemini API, gemini-3-flash-preview, supports function calling and offers enhanced capabilities such as improved reasoning and agentic coding. Developers can define function declarations within the API, allowing the model to interact with external tools seamlessly. When implementing function calling, it is crucial to define clear function declarations and ensure that the model understands the parameters required for each function. Best practices include grouping functions by business meaning and assigning them to agents, which simplifies the management of a large number of functions. Additionally, developers should verify the output of the model to ensure accuracy and reliability Optimize for Scalability with Large Function Sets #\n\nCommon Mistake:\nAvoid mixing unrelated functions in the same module, as it can lead to confusion and increased maintenance costs.\nRobust error handling is essential to ensure the reliability of function calls, especially when interacting with external tools. LLMs should be prepared to handle exceptions gracefully, such as API failures, authentication errors, or unexpected responses. The Haystack tutorial (version 2.4) demonstrates how the rag_pipeline_func includes try-except blocks and logging to manage errors effectively.\nExample:\n\ndef rag_pipeline_func(query):\n    try:\n        response = external_api_call(query)\n        return process_response(response)\n    except AuthenticationError as e:\n        log.error(f\"Authentication failed: {e}\")\n        return \"Error: Authentication failed.\"\n    except APIError as e:\n        log.error(f\"API error: {e}\")\n        return \"Error: Could not retrieve data.\"\n\n\nVerification:\nUse unit tests to simulate error conditions and ensure that the function behaves as expected.\nCommon Mistake:\nIgnoring error types or not providing user feedback can lead to poor user experiences and system instability.\nAs the number of functions increases, especially in large-scale implementations, scalability becomes a critical concern. Grouping functions by business meaning, as recommended in GitHub discussions (issue #9947), helps reduce complexity and improve performance. For systems with thousands of functions, categorization ensures that LLMs can efficiently determine which functions are relevant to a given task. Use Caching for Frequently Accessed Data #\n\nCommon Mistake:\nIgnoring error types or not providing user feedback can lead to poor user experiences and system instability.\nAs the number of functions increases, especially in large-scale implementations, scalability becomes a critical concern. Grouping functions by business meaning, as recommended in GitHub discussions (issue #9947), helps reduce complexity and improve performance. For systems with thousands of functions, categorization ensures that LLMs can efficiently determine which functions are relevant to a given task.\nExample:\n\n# task_grouping.py\nclass TaskGroup:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.functions = []\n\n    def add_function(self, function):\n        self.functions.append(function)\n\n    def get_relevant_functions(self, query):\n        # Use NLP or predefined rules to filter functions\n        return [f for f in self.functions if f.is_relevant(query)]\n\n\nVerification:\nUse performance benchmarks to evaluate the impact of function grouping on response times and memory usage.\nCommon Mistake:\nAvoid overcomplicating grouping logic, as it can lead to increased computational overhead.\nCaching mechanisms can significantly improve the performance of function calling by reducing redundant computations. Storing the results of expensive function calls, such as database queries or API requests, ensures that subsequent calls are served from the cache. In the set_light_values function example, caching is implemented using a decorator or a dedicated caching library like Redis or Memcached. The latest version of the Gemini API, gemini-3-flash-preview, supports function calling and offers enhanced capabilities such as improved reasoning and agentic coding. Developers can define function declarations within the API, allowing the model to interact with external tools seamlessly. Best Practices for Function Calling #. When implementing function calling, it is crucial to define clear function declarations and ensure that the model understands the parameters required for each function. Best practices include grouping functions by business meaning and assigning them to agents, which simplifies the management of a large number of functions. Additionally, developers should verify the output of the model to ensure accuracy and reliability", "providers": ["brave", "ddg"], "provider_count": 2, "score": 0.031754032258064516, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.030834914611005692, "cross_relevance_score": 0.92067564, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.031754032258064516}, {"title": "Function Calling最佳实践2026：从Schema设计到安全防护的完整指南 - ...", "link": "https://dev.to/jiade/function-callingzui-jia-shi-jian-2026cong-schemashe-ji-dao-an-quan-fang-hu-de-wan-zheng-zhi-nan-2ei8", "snippet": "Mar 2, 2026 · Function Call ing 是AI Agent的\"手\"。 如果说LLM的推理能力是\"大脑\"，那么 Fun ction Call ing 就是让AI从\"只会说\"变成\"能做事\"的关键能力。 本文从Schema设计到安全防护，全面解析 20 26 年 的最佳 实践 。 一、 Fun ction Call ing 的本质 1.1 不只是\"调API\"", "providers": ["ddg"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.02309836718438869, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.015151515151515152, "cross_relevance_score": 0.88387895, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.02309836718438869}, {"title": "LLM Function Calling: Complete Implementation Guide (2026)", "link": "https://blog.premai.io/llm-function-calling-complete-implementation-guide-2026/", "snippet": "Summary\n\nModels degrade with too many tools. Guidelines:\nFor large tool sets, implement tool search: the model first calls a search tool to find relevant tools, then those tools are loaded for use.\nNever let the model construct arbitrary code or database queries. Treat tool arguments as untrusted input:\n\n# BAD: SQL injection risk\ndef search_users(query: str):\n    return db.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE '%{query}%'\")\n\n# GOOD: Parameterized query\ndef search_users(query: str):\n    return db.execute(\n        \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name LIKE ?\",\n        [f\"%{query}%\"]\n    )\n\n\nValidate all inputs. Limit tool permissions. Log tool calls for audit.\nFor teams building production systems, PremAI provides a unified API across providers with consistent tool calling behavior, plus fine-tuning capabilities to improve tool use accuracy on your specific functions.\nFunction calling extends LLMs from text generation into action. The core pattern is simple: define tools with JSON schemas, check if the model wants to call them, execute locally, return results. LLM Structured Output: From JSON Mode to Self-Hosted Inference (Complete Guide)\n\nNever let the model construct arbitrary code or database queries. Treat tool arguments as untrusted input:\nValidate all inputs. Limit tool permissions. Log tool calls for audit.\nFor teams building production systems, PremAI provides a unified API across providers with consistent tool calling behavior, plus fine-tuning capabilities to improve tool use accuracy on your specific functions.\n\n{\n    \"name\": \"tool_name\",\n    \"description\": \"What the tool does\",\n    \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {...},\n        \"required\": [...]\n    }\n}\n\n\nFunction calling extends LLMs from text generation into action. The core pattern is simple: define tools with JSON schemas, check if the model wants to call them, execute locally, return results.\nImplementation complexity comes from:\nStart simple. One tool, one execution loop, no streaming. Get that working reliably. Add parallel calls when you have independent operations. Add streaming when responsiveness matters. Build multi-step chains when tasks require it. LLM Structured Output: From JSON Mode to Self-Hosted Inference (Complete Guide)\n\nNever let the model construct arbitrary code or database queries. Treat tool arguments as untrusted input:\nValidate all inputs. Limit tool permissions. Log tool calls for audit.\nFor teams building production systems, PremAI provides a unified API across providers with consistent tool calling behavior, plus fine-tuning capabilities to improve tool use accuracy on your specific functions.\n\n{\n    \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"tool_name\",\n        \"description\": \"What the tool does\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {...},\n            \"required\": [...],\n            \"additionalProperties\": False\n        },\n        \"strict\": True\n    }\n}\n\n\nFunction calling extends LLMs from text generation into action. The core pattern is simple: define tools with JSON schemas, check if the model wants to call them, execute locally, return results.\nImplementation complexity comes from:\nStart simple. One tool, one execution loop, no streaming. Get that working reliably. Add parallel calls when you have independent operations. Add streaming when responsiveness matters. Build multi-step chains when tasks require it. Complete Loop\n\nUse JSON Schema features to constrain inputs:\nEnums, min/max values, and nested objects all help the model generate correct parameters.\nOpenAI's function calling uses the tools parameter in chat completions.\n\n# Let model decide (default)\ntool_choice=\"auto\"\n\n# Force a specific tool\ntool_choice={\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_stock_price\"}}\n\n# Prevent tool use entirely\ntool_choice=\"none\"\n\n# Require at least one tool call\ntool_choice=\"required\"\n\n\nSetting strict: True guarantees the model's output matches your schema exactly. No missing required fields, no invalid enum values, no extra properties.\nStrict mode requires additionalProperties: false on all objects in your schema.\nClaude uses a similar pattern with slightly different structure.", "providers": ["brave"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.026356857985087564, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.015384615384615385, "cross_relevance_score": 0.8457387, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.026356857985087564}, {"title": "Tool Use and Function Calling in AI Agents — Standards, Benchmarks, ...", "link": "https://zylos.ai/research/2026-04-07-tool-use-function-calling-standards-benchmarks/", "snippet": "Best practices now codified across IBM, AWS Well-Architected (Generative AI Lens), and the OWASP AI Agent Security Cheat Sheet: Least privilege per tool: Each tool gets only the permissions it needs for its specific function. Separate read/write: Never bundle read and write permissions unless the task explicitly requires both. Explicit user confirmation for high-risk actions: Writes, deletions, network sends, and code execution", "providers": ["brave", "ddg"], "provider_count": 2, "score": 0.029957522915269395, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.030621785881252923, "cross_relevance_score": 0.8358054, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.029957522915269395}, {"title": "8 Best Function Calling Libraries for LLMs [2026]", "link": "https://techsy.io/en/blog/best-function-calling-libraries", "snippet": "{\"title\":\"8 Best Function Calling Libraries for LLMs [2026]\",\"table\":[{\"Rank\":\"1\",\"Tool\":\"Instructor\",\"Type\":\"Abstraction Library\",\"Best For\":\"Structured outputs + validation\",\"Our Rating\":\"9.5/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"2\",\"Tool\":\"Vercel AI SDK\",\"Type\":\"Abstraction Library\",\"Best For\":\"TypeScript / Next.js projects\",\"Our Rating\":\"9/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"3\",\"Tool\":\"LiteLLM\",\"Type\":\"Unified Proxy\",\"Best For\":\"Multi-provider routing\",\"Our Rating\":\"9/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"4\",\"Tool\":\"Tool Platform\",\"Type\":\"Pre-built Tools\",\"Best For\":\"250+ integrations at scale\",\"Our Rating\":\"8.5/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"5\",\"Tool\":\"Mirascope\",\"Type\":\"Abstraction Library\",\"Best For\":\"Type-safe calling + observability\",\"Our Rating\":\"8.5/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"6\",\"Tool\":\"Magentic\",\"Type\":\"Abstraction Library\",\"Best For\":\"Minimal Pythonic API\",\"Our Rating\":\"8/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"7\",\"Tool\":\"Toolhouse\",\"Type\":\"Tool Platform\",\"Best For\":\"Quick agent prototyping\",\"Our Rating\":\"7.5/10\"},{\"Rank\":\"8\",\"Tool\":\"Native SDKs\",\"Type\":\"Direct API\",\"Best For\":\"Single-provider, zero dependencies\",\"Our Rating\":\"7/10\"}],\"caption\":\"Our Rankings at a Glance\",\"headers\":[\"8 Best Function Calling Libraries for LLMs, Ranked [2026]\"]} {\"title\":\"8 Best Function Calling Libraries for LLMs [2026]\",\"table\":[{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"Reliable structured data extraction in Python\",\"Choose\":\"Instructor (no. 1)\",\"Why\":\"Best retry/validation loop, 15+ providers\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"TypeScript or Next.js frontend integration\",\"Choose\":\"Vercel AI SDK (no. 2)\",\"Why\":\"Native TS, React hooks, streaming UI\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"Multi-provider routing for a team\",\"Choose\":\"LiteLLM (no. 3)\",\"Why\":\"100+ providers, cost tracking, failover\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"250+ pre-built third-party integrations\",\"Choose\":\"Composio (no. 4)\",\"Why\":\"Managed OAuth, MCP, agent-ready\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"Production observability with OTel\",\"Choose\":\"Mirascope (no. 5)\",\"Why\":\"Built-in tracing, clean decorator API\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"The most minimal, Pythonic API\",\"Choose\":\"Magentic (no. 6)\",\"Why\":\"@prompt decorator, tiny API surface\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"Fastest path to a working agent demo\",\"Choose\":\"Toolhouse (no. 7)\",\"Why\":\"3-line setup, managed execution\"},{\"If Your Project Needs...\":\"Maximum control, single provider\",\"Choose\":\"Native SDKs (no. 8)\",\"Why\":\"Zero dependencies, full API access\"}],\"caption\":\"Which Function Calling Library Should You Choose?\",\"headers\":[\"8 Best Function Calling Libraries for LLMs, Ranked [2026]\"]} {\"mainEntity\":[{\"name\":\"What is the best library for LLM function calling in 2026?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Instructor is our top pick for Python developers who need reliable structured outputs. For TypeScript, Vercel AI SDK is the clear winner. LiteLLM is best for multi-provider routing, and Composio wins when you need pre-built tool integrations.\"}},{\"name\":\"Should I use native SDKs or a library for function calling?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Use native SDKs only if you're locked to one provider and want absolute control. The moment you need retries on malformed outputs, multi-provider support, or type-safe schemas, a library like Instructor or Mirascope pays for itself in the first week.\"}},{\"name\":\"What is the difference between function calling and tool calling?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"They're the same concept with different names. OpenAI originally called it \\\"function calling,\\\" Anthropic uses \\\"tool use,\\\" and the industry is converging on \\\"tool calling.\\\" The mechanics are identical: the LLM outputs a structured request, your code executes it, and the result goes back to the model.\"}},{\"name\":\"Is LangChain still good for function calling in 2026?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Many developers have moved to lighter alternatives. LangChain works, but its deep abstraction layers add complexity that's overkill if function calling is your primary need. Instructor, Mirascope, and LiteLLM solve the same problem with significantly less overhead and better debugging.\"}},{\"name\":\"What is the difference between Composio and Toolhouse?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Both are tool platforms, but they optimize for different scales. Composio offers 250+ integrations with managed OAuth and enterprise features -- ideal for production agents that touch many services. Toolhouse focuses on simplicity with a 3-line setup, making it better for prototyping and smaller projects.\"}},{\"name\":\"Which function calling library supports the most LLM providers?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"LiteLLM leads with 100+ providers through its OpenAI-compatible proxy. Vercel AI SDK supports 20+ through community adapters. Instructor covers 15+, and Mirascope handles 10+.\"}},{\"name\":\"Can I use Instructor with Anthropic Claude?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Yes. Instructor supports Claude through client patching, along with 14+ other providers including Gemini, Mistral, Cohere, and local models via Ollama. The retry and validation logic works identically across all supported providers.\"}},{\"name\":\"What is MCP and how does it relate to function calling?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is Anthropic's open standard for connecting LLMs to external tools and data sources. It standardizes how tools are discovered and executed. Composio, Toolhouse, and Vercel AI SDK all support MCP servers. Read our MCP complete guide for the full picture.\"}},{\"name\":\"Can I combine multiple function calling libraries?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"Absolutely -- and you should. The most common production stack is LiteLLM for provider routing plus Instructor for validated outputs. Add Composio on top if you need third-party integrations. These tools solve different layers of the problem, so they compose naturally.\"}},{\"name\":\"Do I need function calling for simple chatbots?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"text\":\"No. Function calling adds complexity that's only worth it when your LLM needs to take actions or return structured data. If you're building a Q&A chatbot that just responds with text, the native SDK's chat completion is all you need. Save function calling for when the model needs to interact with external systems.\"}}]} Most real-world projects combine layers. A common stack we use: LiteLLM for provider routing, Instructor on top for validated function calling, and Composio when agents need third-party integrations. Start with what solves your most pressing problem, then layer as needed", "providers": ["brave", "brightdata", "ddg"], "provider_count": 3, "score": 0.031544957774465976, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.044092411739470566, "cross_relevance_score": 0.8187948, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.031544957774465976}, {"title": "ai agent 工具 调用 深度 指南 2026 : function calling 设计 模式 与 最佳 实践 - csdn 博客", "link": "https://blog.csdn.net/yonggeit/article/details/160904067", "snippet": "ai agent 工具 调用 深度 指南 2026 ： function calling 设计 模式 与 最佳 实践 \n 最新 推荐 文章 于 2026 - 07 - 15 18 : 29 : 3 4 发布 \n 原创 于 2026 - 05 - 09 00 : 16 : 23 发布 · 329 阅读 \n function calling 是 ai agent 与 外部 世界 交互 的 核心 接口 ...", "domain": "blog.csdn.net", "providers": ["langsearch"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.024696969696969696, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.015873015873015872, "cross_relevance_score": 0.78962326, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.024696969696969696}, {"title": "Function Calling Best Practices: How to Build AI Agents That Use Tools Reliably | by Anna Jey | Toward Next AI | Apr, 2026 | Medium", "link": "https://medium.com/toward-next-ai/function-calling-best-practices-how-to-build-ai-agents-that-use-tools-reliably-3c93d8e9cef7", "snippet": "May 25, 2026 - When a tool-calling workflow fails, developers usually ask the wrong first question. ... Without this, you are trying to debug a chain of decisions from memory. That never goes well. A response can sound good and still have a broken execution path. That is why you should score tool use directly. ... This is the shift many teams need to make in 2026.", "providers": ["brave", "ddg"], "provider_count": 2, "score": 0.02904040404040404, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.029957522915269395, "cross_relevance_score": 0.77635175, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.02904040404040404}, {"title": "LLM Function Calling in 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Structured Outputs", "link": "https://futureagi.com/blog/llm-function-calling-2025/", "snippet": "OpenAI tool use in 2026\n\nExample tool definition (provider neutral):\nThree rules of thumb for tool schemas:\nOpenAI’s Responses API is the recommended path. A minimal tool-use call:\n\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ntools = [\n    {\n        \"type\": \"function\",\n        \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n        \"description\": \"Look up the current status of a customer order by order ID.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\n                \"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n            },\n            \"required\": [\"order_id\"],\n            \"additionalProperties\": False,\n        },\n    }\n]\n\nresponse = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    input=\"What is the status of order A-94821?\",\n    tools=tools,\n    tool_choice=\"auto\",\n)\n\nfor item in response.output:\n    if item.type == \"function_call\":\n        print(item.name, item.arguments)\n\nSet tool_choice to auto (model decides), required (model must call something), none (no tools this turn), or {\"type\": \"function\", \"name\": \"...\"} to force a specific tool.\nFor guaranteed-parseable responses, use Structured Outputs. The exact SDK method (for example responses.parse, responses.create with text.format, or beta.chat.completions.parse) shifts across SDK versions, so always check the OpenAI Python SDK release notes for the snapshot you target. The conceptual pattern is the same: define a Pydantic model or JSON Schema and pass it as the response schema.\nAnthropic’s Messages API uses an almost-identical shape: Anthropic Claude tool use\n\nOpenAI’s Responses API is the recommended path. A minimal tool-use call:\nSet tool_choice to auto (model decides), required (model must call something), none (no tools this turn), or {\"type\": \"function\", \"name\": \"...\"} to force a specific tool.\nFor guaranteed-parseable responses, use Structured Outputs. The exact SDK method (for example responses.parse, responses.create with text.format, or beta.chat.completions.parse) shifts across SDK versions, so always check the OpenAI Python SDK release notes for the snapshot you target. The conceptual pattern is the same: define a Pydantic model or JSON Schema and pass it as the response schema.\n\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclass OrderQuery(BaseModel):\n    order_id: str\n    include_history: bool = False\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n# Check the current OpenAI Python SDK docs for the exact structured-output\n# helper that matches your installed version, then pass OrderQuery to it.\n\nAnthropic’s Messages API uses an almost-identical shape:\nClaude tool_choice options: {\"type\": \"auto\"}, {\"type\": \"any\"}, {\"type\": \"none\"}, or {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"...\"}. Set disable_parallel_tool_use=True to force serial execution.\nAfter running the tool, send the result back in the next user message: LLM function calling is the bridge from words to actions, and in 2026 it is the foundation of every serious LLM product. Master the JSON Schema contract, pick the provider features that match your needs (structured outputs, parallel calls, tool_choice), and lock in continuous evaluation and tracing so the agent that worked last week still works this week Structured outputs are a hard-typed extension of function calling. Instead of letting the model decide which tool to call, you constrain the entire response to match a JSON Schema or Pydantic model. OpenAI calls this Structured Outputs and supports it on Responses and Chat Completions. Gemini calls it response_schema. Anthropic supports structured JSON-style outputs through prompting and tool schemas, so check the current Messages API docs for the strict schema guarantees on your snapshot. Use structured outputs when you want zero free-form text and guaranteed parse-ability. ... Parallel tool calls let the model emit multiple tool requests in a single response, useful when the requested actions are independent. OpenAI and Anthropic both expose this. The caller fans out the tool executions, waits for all results, then sends them back as a batched tool_results message", "providers": ["brave"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.027579737335834898, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.015151515151515152, "cross_relevance_score": 0.7688026, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.027579737335834898}, {"title": "LLM Function Calling: Structured Outputs Guide 2026", "link": "https://blog.newtum.com/llm-function-calling-structured-outputs/", "snippet": "{\"title\":\"LLM Function Calling: Structured Outputs Guide 2026\",\"table\":[{\"Scenario\":\"User triggers a specific backend action\",\"Best Approach\":\"Function calling\"},{\"Scenario\":\"Extracting structured data from a document\",\"Best Approach\":\"Structured outputs\"},{\"Scenario\":\"Multi-step agent with branching logic\",\"Best Approach\":\"Function calling with multiple tools\"},{\"Scenario\":\"Building a form filler or data parser\",\"Best Approach\":\"Structured outputs\"},{\"Scenario\":\"Real-time API integration (booking, ordering)\",\"Best Approach\":\"Function calling\"},{\"Scenario\":\"Classification or entity extraction\",\"Best Approach\":\"Structured outputs\"}],\"caption\":\"When to Use Which Approach\",\"headers\":[\"Stop Writing Chatbots: How LLM function calling structured outputs Connect AI to Real APIs\"]} Claude’s Approach: Tool Use\n\nNotice how tool_choice=\"auto\" lets the model decide whether to call a function or respond conversationally. If you set tool_choice={\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_weather\"}}, you force it to always call that specific function—a stricter form of LLM function calling and structured outputs that removes ambiguity entirely.\nWhen you do not need function routing and just want guaranteed JSON shape, use OpenAI’s Structured Outputs feature with response_format:\npython\n\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nclass FlightDetails(BaseModel):\n    destination: str\n    date: str\n    passengers: int\n\nresponse = client.beta.chat.completions.parse(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Book me a flight to New York tomorrow for 2 people\"}\n    ],\n    response_format=FlightDetails,\n)\n\nflight = response.choices[0].message.parsed\nprint(flight.destination)  # NYC\nprint(flight.date)         # 2026-05-19\nprint(flight.passengers)   # 2\n\nUsing Pydantic models with parse() is the cleanest way to work with LLM function calling and structured outputs in Python—the SDK validates the schema automatically and raises an error if the model produces invalid output.\nGoogle Gemini handles LLM function calling through its function_declarations parameter in the API configuration. The pattern is nearly identical to OpenAI’s but uses slightly different naming conventions.\npython Common Mistakes Junior Developers Make\n\nUsing Pydantic models with parse() is the cleanest way to work with LLM function calling and structured outputs in Python—the SDK validates the schema automatically and raises an error if the model produces invalid output.\nGoogle Gemini handles LLM function calling through its function_declarations parameter in the API configuration. The pattern is nearly identical to OpenAI’s but uses slightly different naming conventions.\npython\n\nimport google.generativeai as genai\n\ngenai.configure(api_key=\"YOUR_KEY\")\n\ntools = genai.protos.Tool(\n    function_declarations=[\n        genai.protos.FunctionDeclaration(\n            name=\"search_product\",\n            description=\"Search for a product in the catalog\",\n            parameters=genai.protos.Schema(\n                type=genai.protos.Type.OBJECT,\n                properties={\n                    \"query\": genai.protos.Schema(type=genai.protos.Type.STRING),\n                    \"max_price\": genai.protos.Schema(type=genai.protos.Type.NUMBER),\n                },\n                required=[\"query\"]\n            )\n        )\n    ]\n)\n\nmodel = genai.GenerativeModel(\"gemini-1.5-pro\", tools=[tools])\nresponse = model.generate_content(\"Find headphones under ₹5000\")\n\n# Extract the function call\nfc = response.candidates[0].content.parts[0].function_call\nprint(dict(fc.args))\n# Output: {\"query\": \"headphones\", \"max_price\": 5000}\n\nGemini also supports a response_mime_type: \"application/json\" parameter combined with a schema prompt for structured outputs without function declarations—useful for data extraction tasks.\nAnthropic’s Claude uses the term tool use instead of function calling, but the concept is identical. You pass a tools array with each tool’s input schema, and Claude returns a tool_use content block when it decides a tool should be invoked.\npython from openai import OpenAI from pydantic import BaseModel client = OpenAI() class FlightDetails(BaseModel): destination: str date: str passengers: int response = client.beta.chat.completions.parse( model=\"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\", messages=[ {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Book me a flight to New York tomorrow for 2 people\"} ], response_format=FlightDetails, ) flight = response.choices.message.parsed print(flight.destination) # NYC print(flight.date) # 2026-05-19 print(flight.passengers) # 2 ·", "providers": ["brave"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.03036576949620428, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.01639344262295082, "cross_relevance_score": 0.7667131, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.03036576949620428}, {"title": "Tool (aka Function Calling) Best Practices | by Laurent Kubaski | Medium", "link": "https://medium.com/@laurentkubaski/tool-or-function-calling-best-practices-a5165a33d5f1", "snippet": "January 1, 2026 - I also wrote a follow-up article called Tool Calling Using the OpenAI Python SDK: Full Example With Best Practices. ... This is a follow up to my previous “Ollama Tool support” article: in that article I wrote “function calling with small Ollama models starts breaking”… but what happens when using the OpenAI GPT-4 models like GPT-4.1-nano, GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4.1-mini or GPT-4.1 ?", "providers": ["brightdata", "ddg"], "provider_count": 2, "score": 0.029631255487269532, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.029513888888888888, "cross_relevance_score": 0.7667131, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.029631255487269532}, {"title": "LLM Function Calling and Tool Use Guide 2026: OpenAI ...", "link": "https://baeseokjae.github.io/posts/llm-function-calling-tool-use-guide-2026/", "snippet": "Apr 27, 2026 · Complete 2026 guide to LLM function calling across OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google Gemini—with code, security, and production patterns.", "providers": ["ddg"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.026153846153846153, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.015384615384615385, "cross_relevance_score": 0.74510175, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.026153846153846153}, {"title": "Tool & Function Calling - Use Tools with OpenRouter", "link": "https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/features/tool-calling", "snippet": "When defining tools for LLMs, follow these best practices: Clear and Descriptive Names: Use descriptive function names that clearly indicate the tool’s purpose. // Good: Clear and specific { \"name\": \"get_weather_forecast\" }. // Avoid: Too vague { \"name\": \"weather\" }. Comprehensive Descriptions: Provide detailed descriptions that help the model understand when and how to use the tool. { \"description\": \"Get current weather conditions and 5-day forecast for a specific location … Here are the essential request body formats for each step: ###. Step 1: Inference Request with Tools. { \"model\": \"google/gemini-3-flash-preview\", \"messages\": [ { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What are the titles of some James Joyce books?\" } ], \"tools\": [ { \"type\": \"function\", \"function\": { \"name\": \"search_gutenberg_books\", \"description\": \"Search for books in the Project Gutenberg library\", \"parameters\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { \"search_terms\": { \"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"description\": \"List of search terms to find books\" } }, \"required\": [\"search_terms\"] } } } ] }. See all 29 lines", "providers": ["brave"], "provider_count": 1, "score": 0.027146130794993832, "provider_consensus_rrf_score": 0.014285714285714285, "cross_relevance_score": 0.7383674, "hybrid_rrf_score": 0.027146130794993832}, {"title": "Function Calling Best Practices | Fewzen AI Documentation", "link": "https://docs.fewzen.ai/function-calling/best-practices", "snippet": "f